      <Comment source="pmngroup">APS kinases are small diffusible proteins. All APS kinases that had been studied in vitro exhibited a very high substrate affinity for APS with a Km in the micromolar range. APS kinases are substrate inhibited by APS. Plant APS kinases differ from those of bacteria or fungal by their high contents of cysteine residues. The Arabidopsis genome contains four APS kinases, two of them have been shown functional active. However, since they are both predicted to be located in the plastid, where the product PAPS has an unknown function. PAPS are sulfate donors in sulfation reactions which occur in the cytoplasm. Sulfate esters include such as sulfated flavonols, glucosinolates, plant steroids and phytosulfokines. In vitro assay of the Arabidopsis Akn1 APS kinase showed that thioredoxin is an essential activator. The Arabidopsis Akn1 and Akn2 interact with each other in a yeast two-hybrid system, and possibly form a heterodimer.</Comment>
